{"id": "sevt_01RJ739KecMFFysch742SeJK", "content": [{"text": "I triaged F-101 and created one ticket draft. The SQL injection is real and exploitable without logging in: `GET /users?name=' OR '1'='1` returns every user's id and email, and a UNION query could read other tables.\n\n| ID | Rule | Location | Verdict | Severity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F-101 | SQL Injection | app/routes/users.py:15 | exploitable | high |\n\n**How the input reaches the query:**\n- The `name` value comes straight from the URL's query string (`app/routes/users.py:12`).\n- It is pasted into the SQL text without any escaping (`app/routes/users.py:14`).\n- That query is then run and all matching rows are sent back to the caller (`app/routes/users.py:15-16`).\n- The `/users` route has no login check, and nothing else in the repository adds one.\n\n**One gap:** the code that plugs this route into the running app isn't in the uploaded repository. I couldn't confirm the final URL or rule out an auth layer added outside this code. Based on what's here, I treated the route as reachable.\n\n**Fix:** use a parameterized query, for example `conn.execute(\"... WHERE name = ?\", (name,))`, and add a test that sends an injection payload.\n\n**Separate issue (not ticketed):** `config.py:4` has a cloud access key written directly into the source. I left the value out of the ticket and this summary. The key should be rotated and moved to a secret store, and it needs its own ticket.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:52:03.975496Z", "type": "agent.message"}
